      <h1 class="mb-8 text-balance">The Architecture of Commitment</h1>

      <div class="accent-line mb-8"></div>

      <div class="bg-oat-100 p-6 mb-12 space-y-4">
        <p class="text-ink-light">
          <span class="text-ink font-medium">For people:</span> Know what to commit to and what to leave open. Lock in your values; stay flexible on methods. As you get better at measuring results, the hard question shifts from "am I doing this right?" to "is this the right thing to do?"
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink-light">
          <span class="text-ink font-medium">For organizations:</span> As measurement improves, variance shifts from execution to objective selection. The strategic question becomes "are we doing the right things?" instead of "are we doing things right?" Organizations that confuse ends and means—or treat both the same way—either drift aimlessly or calcify around outdated methods.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink-light text-sm pt-2 border-t border-oat-300">
          If these questions resonate, <a href="/contact" class="text-sage-700 hover:text-sage-900 underline">I'd welcome the conversation</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg">
        <p>A university president I know inherited a crisis. National rankings had slipped for five straight years. The previous leadership's response was to revise priorities based on whatever metric dropped most—student selectivity one year, research output the next, alumni giving after that. Each pivot came with new initiatives, new hires, new dashboards. Administrators were exhausted. Nothing improved.</p>

        <p>Her solution seemed counterintuitive: she stopped chasing rankings. Instead, she made one commitment explicit and non-negotiable—students would leave with the ability to think clearly about hard problems. That was locked in. But how to achieve it—pedagogy, requirements, extracurriculars—remained open to constant revision as evidence accumulated about what actually worked. The cost of that commitment was real. Locking in "clear thinking" meant saying no to initiatives that would have boosted short-term metrics. A proposed partnership with a certification provider would have increased job placement rates (a ranked metric) but reduced curricular flexibility. A push to increase enrollment would have improved revenue and visibility but diluted the seminar-intensive model that made the thinking happen. Faculty who had built careers around publishable research felt sidelined when the institution stopped rewarding citation counts. Some left. The alumni board, trained to interpret rankings as report cards, needed repeated explanation of why their dollars were being spent on something that couldn't be benchmarked against peer institutions.</p>

        <p>The distinction she drew is, I think, the central architectural problem organizations face. The commitment to clear thinking should be stable—not revised every time a ranking methodology changes. But the specific methods should adapt constantly as evidence accumulates. Organizations that treat both the same way either drift aimlessly, revising their purposes whenever the numbers dip, or calcify, refusing to update outdated methods because "that's how we do things." Within four years, her university was climbing again. The difference was knowing which questions should stay open and which should stay closed.</p>

        <h2>The Measurement Paradox</h2>

        <p>As organizations get better at measuring things, this architectural problem shifts. Better data makes execution questions tractable. Did the marketing campaign increase sales? Did the new process reduce defects? Did the training program improve retention? With good data, these questions have answers. But better measurement does not resolve the question of whether the right things are being measured in the first place. A hospital can measure patient throughput with extraordinary precision, but that precision does not tell anyone whether throughput should be prioritized over patient experience, or whether either should matter more than staff wellbeing. I call this the measurement paradox: as the ability to track outcomes improves, the question "are we doing things right?" becomes answerable, while the question "are we doing the right things?" becomes more exposed as the contested domain that data alone cannot settle.</p>

        <p>Consider climate metrics as an illustration. Thirty years ago, most organizations could not measure their carbon footprint with any reliability. Both the question "are we reducing emissions effectively?" and the question "should we prioritize emissions reduction?" were bundled together in general uncertainty. Now, measurement technology has separated them. We can track emissions precisely, evaluate reduction strategies rigorously, and assign credit or blame for outcomes. But that precision has not resolved whether emissions reduction should rank above shareholder returns, employee wages, or product affordability when they conflict. Better data exposes the choice of what to optimize as the question requiring human judgment. By "human judgment" I mean the political and institutional processes through which stakeholders with competing values arrive at provisional commitments about what matters.</p>

        <h2>How Objectives Stabilize</h2>

        <p>If objectives cannot be validated through measurement, what holds them in place? Why do organizations not constantly renegotiate their purposes? The answer involves structure. In any organization, some positions influence others more than they are influenced in return. A CEO's stated priorities shape what employees attend to more than employee preferences shape the CEO's priorities. This asymmetry arises from position: their role creates a stable reference point that enables coordinated action. When everyone in a room can influence everyone else equally, beliefs converge toward mushy averages that no one firmly holds, and coordinated action becomes difficult because there is no clear focal point. But when some positions are structurally insulated from continuous influence by others, their commitments remain clear enough to give everyone else direction.</p>

        <p>T-Mobile provides a striking example. For years, the company maintained a single, stable commitment: be the lowest-cost carrier. That commitment was specific enough to coordinate action across thousands of employees and stable enough to survive quarterly pressure to raise prices slightly and capture more margin. When competitors offered premium pricing for better networks, T-Mobile didn't chase the margin. When analysts questioned why they weren't monetizing their customer base more aggressively, management explained: they were waiting. The strategy required maintaining a price gap until network quality could catch up—at which point they could offer both the best value and the best performance. That moment came after the blocked AT&T merger in 2011. T-Mobile received breakup fees and valuable low-band spectrum that dramatically improved their coverage. They had maintained their commitment through years of apparent disadvantage, and the investment paid off.</p>

        <p>The leader needs to be stable enough that others can explore different approaches to achieving the stated objectives, and that exploration eventually reveals whether those objectives were productive. If outcomes are persistently poor despite good execution, the leader learns from results and can revise. The revision happens through evidence accumulated over time, through the cadence of results instead of the averaging of opinions that would have destabilized commitment before evidence could accumulate. A clarification: by "stable" I mean that the revision of objectives follows a different cadence and different triggers than the revision of methods—a distinction the next section develops.</p>

        <h2>Ends and Means</h2>

        <p>This points toward a distinction that I think determines how objectives should be governed. Some objectives specify what the organization is trying to achieve—end-oriented objectives that express values. "We exist to improve human health." "Our purpose is delivering value to shareholders." "We prioritize sustainable practices." These cannot be verified by measurement because they define what success means. They are evaluative commitments. Chester Barnard made this point forcefully in 1938: "The objective purpose of no organization is profit, but services." Profit may be essential—a necessary condition for continuing to provide inducements to contributors—but it is not the purpose. Organizations that confuse a necessary condition with a purpose lose the ability to distinguish what they are optimizing <em>for</em> from what they are optimizing <em>with</em>.</p>

        <p>Other objectives specify how to achieve those ends—means-oriented objectives that express methods. "We will reduce costs through process automation." "We will increase revenue through geographic expansion." "We will improve safety through standardized protocols." These can be tested against outcomes. If automation does not reduce costs, that is evidence against the method. The governance implication is direct: means-oriented objectives should be adaptive, revised when evidence suggests they are not working; end-oriented objectives should be stable, revised only when the values they express no longer represent what stakeholders care about. An organization that revises its purpose every quarter drifts. An organization that never revises its methods calcifies.</p>

        <h2>Three Levels of Architecture</h2>

        <p>This distinction suggests organizations need architecture at three levels, each governed differently. At the constitutional level, organizations settle foundational questions: Who counts as a stakeholder? Whose interests deserve consideration? What can and cannot be traded off? These are fundamentally questions about values. A pharmaceutical company that decides patients' interests take precedence over shareholders' when the two conflict has made a constitutional commitment; so has one that decides the opposite. Neither decision can be validated by performance metrics—both are evaluative settlements that shape everything downstream. At the institutional level, organizations translate constitutional commitments into priorities. Given that patients matter, which patient interests are most salient? Safety? Access? Affordability? Privacy? Constitutional commitment to patients does not specify how to weigh these competing interests—that is institutional work, more revisable than constitutional commitments but more stable than day-to-day operations. At the operational level, organizations figure out how to execute. Given that patient safety is the priority, what protocols achieve it? This is where measurement is most useful and where adaptation should be most continuous.</p>

        <p>The architecture determines what kind of feedback revises what kind of objective. Poor quarterly results should trigger operational review—are our methods effective? Persistent poor results despite operational adjustment should trigger institutional review—are our priorities right? Systematic failure to serve stakeholders should trigger constitutional review—do our foundational commitments still reflect what we and our stakeholders value? A clarification: this hierarchy offers an analytic framework for diagnosing when the wrong level is being revised. Most organizations blur these levels constantly, and the blurring is itself the source of the characteristic failures.</p>

        <h2>When Constitutions Conflict</h2>

        <p>The hardest cases arise when constitutional commitments themselves conflict—when an organization discovers that its foundational values are genuinely incommensurable. Consider what happens when an organization's commitment to serving current customers conflicts with its commitment to building the company that can serve future customers. A technology company facing this choice—cannibalize the profitable legacy business to fund an uncertain future, or preserve what works until it doesn't—cannot resolve it through measurement. Both commitments are constitutional. Both are sincerely held. And both cannot be fully honored simultaneously. There is no algorithm for this. The organization faces a choice that its own constitution cannot make: which constitutional commitment takes precedence when both cannot be honored?</p>

        <p>This is the condition of having multiple values in a world where they sometimes conflict. The best an organization can do is recognize when it faces a constitutional conflict instead of an institutional or operational one—and understand that resolving it requires political negotiation among stakeholders.</p>

        <h2>The Strategic Question Shifts</h2>

        <p>As measurement improves, the strategic frontier shifts. When measurement was primitive, strategy concerned execution—how to build an organization that pursues its objectives effectively. That question remains important, but better measurement makes execution increasingly tractable. Organizations converge on effective practices through observation, imitation, and explicit benchmarking, and competitive advantage from execution erodes as best practices diffuse. What remains contested is what the organization should be pursuing in the first place—which stakeholders to serve, which dimensions of performance to prioritize, which tradeoffs to accept. The strategic question becomes "what should we be pursuing?" and, at the highest level, "how should we structure our ability to change direction when our current objectives no longer serve our purposes?"</p>

        <p>Organizations that have not explicitly distinguished these levels risk two characteristic failures. The first is drift—without constitutional commitments shielded from ordinary performance feedback, the organization migrates toward whatever its measurement systems make visible. Shareholder value became the de facto purpose of many corporations through the drift of attention toward what was most measurable and reportable. The second is rigidity—without operational premises that adapt to evidence, organizations calcify around methods that worked historically but no longer serve current conditions, and the commitment to "how we do things" gets confused with the commitment to "what we are trying to achieve." Good architecture prevents both by specifying what is stable, what is adaptive, and what mechanisms govern transition between them.</p>

        <p>But architecture cannot prevent constitutional conflicts from arising. And when they do, the question shifts to politics: "who gets to decide when our deepest commitments cannot all be honored?" I am not sure any organization has solved this problem fully. The ones that navigate it best seem to do so through explicit commitment at the constitutional level combined with restless experimentation at the operational level—and the discipline to know which level a given challenge belongs to.</p>
      </div>

