        <h3 class="text-lg font-medium mb-4">Notes</h3>
        <ol class="text-sm text-ink-light space-y-3 list-decimal pl-5">
          <li id="fn1">Chester Barnard's <em>The Functions of the Executive</em> (1938) remains foundational for understanding how organizations maintain coherent purpose. His insight that "the objective purpose of no organization is profit, but services" clarifies the distinction between what organizations optimize <em>for</em> and what they optimize <em>with</em>.</li>
          <li id="fn2">The three-level architecture I propose—constitutional, institutional, operational—is my synthesis of ideas from constitutional economics (Buchanan), institutional theory (North, Meyer and Rowan), and organizational design. The key insight is that different levels require different governance mechanisms.</li>
          <li id="fn3">T-Mobile's strategic evolution is reconstructed from public sources including SEC filings, earnings calls, and industry analysis. The pattern of maintaining commitment through apparent disadvantage—waiting for circumstances to validate the strategy—appears across successful long-term performers.</li>
          <li id="fn4">Constitutional conflicts—when foundational commitments to different stakeholders cannot all be honored simultaneously—expose the limits of architecture. Structure cannot resolve these tensions; they require political negotiation among stakeholders.</li>
          <li id="fn5">The distinction between "drift" (revising purpose too easily) and "rigidity" (revising methods too slowly) is my framing of a classic organizational pathology. Good architecture prevents both by specifying what is stable, what is adaptive, and what triggers transitions between them.</li>
        </ol>
